I just pushed a fix for the command-line Stan that
enabled prints in the generated quantities block.

Jiqiang -- could you fix it for RStan?

In the command-line code, I had to change

     model.write_csv(params_r,params_i,sample_file_stream);

to

     model.write_csv(params_r,params_i,sample_file_stream,&std::cout);

in two places (warmup and sampling calls) to pass in a pointer
to the std output stream.  The default was to set the print
pointer to 0 and not print.

Assuming this hasn't been done yet, RStan needs to pass a pointer
to the R debug print stream -- the same one that gets fed to the model
for printing.

Here's a simple test model that's not printing now in
the currently released RStan:

--------------------
parameters {
   real y;
}
model {
   y ~ normal(0,1);
   print("model y=",y);
}
generated quantities {
   real z;
   z <- 2 * y;
   print("gq z=",z);
}
-----------------

- Bob

The valgrind output Jiqiang let Daniel and I find the
leak right away.  The problem is the use of Eigen::Matrix
as a member variable in vari specializations.

Like std::vector, Eigen::Matrix allocates the memory for
its contents on the heap.  Because it was in a vari,
its destructor was never getting called.

To fix the problem, we used our own arena-based memory
manager to allocate an array, then wrapped it in a Map
locally.

We also added gradient tests for determinant and log_determinant,
(they passed, so the original implementation was good).

Here's the fixed version of determinant (less the function
that didn't need to be fixed):

     template<int R,int C>
     class determinant_vari : public vari {
       int _rows;
       int _cols;
       double* _A;
       vari** _adjARef;
     public:
       determinant_vari(const Eigen::Matrix<var,R,C> &A)
       : vari(determinant_vari_calc(A)),
         _rows(A.rows()),
         _cols(A.cols()),
         _A((double*)stan::agrad::memalloc_.alloc(sizeof(double)
                                                  * A.rows() * A.cols())),
         _adjARef((vari**)stan::agrad::memalloc_.alloc(sizeof(vari*)
                                                       * A.rows() * A.cols()))
       {
         size_t pos = 0;
         for (size_type j = 0; j < _cols; j++) {
           for (size_type i = 0; i < _rows; i++) {
             _A[pos] = A(i,j).val();
             _adjARef[pos++] = A(i,j).vi_;
           }
         }
       }
...
       virtual void chain() {
         using Eigen::Matrix;
         using Eigen::Map;
         Matrix<double,R,C> adjA(_rows,_cols);
         adjA = (adj_ * val_) *
           Map<Matrix<double,R,C> >(_A,_rows,_cols).inverse().transpose();
         size_t pos = 0;
         for (size_type j = 0; j < _cols; j++) {
           for (size_type i = 0; i < _rows; i++) {
             _adjARef[pos++]->adj_ += adjA(i,j);
           }
         }
       }
     };

- Bob

Ah, good catch.  Thanks for tracking this down!

Cheers,
